# d3 — Odhrán on the river bank
#
#   node tools/dialogue/compile.mjs tools/dialogue/drafts/d3.dlg --check
#   node tools/dialogue/compile.mjs tools/dialogue/drafts/d3.dlg
#
# Built from the SHORT draft. The earlier long version is in the git history
# at 753e9f7 as tools/dialogue/drafts/c3Ford.dlg, along with its skeleton at
# public/data/bog/c3.js. Both are removed by this change: the encounter lives
# on d3 now, and c3 is free again.
#
# ── STRUCTURE ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# A LINEAR chain, not a hub. Every node advances on any non-exit option.
# This is deliberate: hold:true never advances npcProgress (encounterPanel
# 485/571/641), so a held node is TERMINAL -- the player stays on it and
# nothing after it is ever reachable. The hub shape only works if the held
# node's own @when eventually fails. This conversation is one unbroken
# thread, so a chain is both simpler and safe.
#
#   0  three hails, no answer      7  the rock
#   1  are you hale                8  the binding
#   2  struck by words             9  what am I now
#   3  name your toll             10  the rope
#   4  the feast, and a bard      11  let me help
#   5  upon whom                  12  afterwards
#   6  the widow's bread
#
# Every node carries an exit, so the player can leave at any depth.
#
# ── PLACEMENT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# (19,12) -- the LAST tile of the briugu's old walk path. The previous d3.js
# header recorded it as verified against the tile layer: (12,5)-(18,11)
# grass, (19,12) the waterside strip where bank meets channel. Already
# proven reachable from the boat, which is why it is worth reusing rather
# than picking a fresh tile.
#
# He is LYING DOWN and does not get up. If the sprite ends up standing, node
# 0 reads wrong -- the whole opening beat is that the toll-man does not rise.
#
# He is on a river the player is ROWING, not a ford they wade, so the rope
# goes across the CHANNEL. That works harder here than it did at a crossing:
# a rope across the water is the thing that would actually stop a boat.
#
# ── WHAT THIS REPLACED ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The walking briugu. His full text, Irish intact, is parked at
# tools/dialogue/drafts/d3Briugu.dlg -- 12 translated lines, which were the
# only finished Irish in the whole cold open. Nothing loads that file.
#
# TWO THINGS THE SWAP COSTS, both worth weighing before this is called done:
#
# 1. met_briugu IS NOW DEAD. Only the briugu ever set it, and b0 still gates
#    Mór's road line on it -- "I saw a man on the road, going east. He would
#    not say why." The linter reports it as "required but never set". It does
#    NOT transfer as written: Odhrán is stationary, tells everyone
#    everything, and keeps a toll rather than a house. Re-gating it on
#    met_odhran needs both of Mór's lines rewritten, and that is Irish, so it
#    is yours. Something like "I saw a man by the water who could not name
#    his own toll" would keep her "..." doing the same work.
#
# 2. THE PACING ARGUMENT. The old d3.js header held that this beat's job was
#    PACING and its encounter should be "deliberately thin" -- because if the
#    thing after Muireann is another conversation, the game reads as a
#    dialogue tree with scenery between the nodes. Odhrán is now the longest
#    encounter in the project. His MAINLINE is short (three hails, a wound,
#    and away) and all the depth is opt-in, which is the defence. But the
#    argument was a good one.
#
# What DOES survive the swap is the briugu's other job: establishing an NPC
# who does not want to talk, so the villagers' evasiveness later reads as
# character rather than the game withholding. Odhrán's opening silence does
# that harder than the briugu's terseness did.
#
# ── THE SILENCE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Node 0's hails each get "..." as the reply rather than no reply at all.
# An empty reply trips the linter's "speaks but gets no answer" -- and more
# to the point, a card containing only "..." IS the beat. The player is made
# to sit in the silence rather than have it skipped past.
#
# ── THE BYPASS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# has_druid_word (d3Sea node 4) opens the bypass on node 0: passage granted,
# free, having learned none of this -- exactly what Muireann told them to do,
# "You need say no more than that. And say no more." @first, so it does not
# nag on later visits.
#
# It is deliberately NOT an @exit option. encounterPanel line 672 reads
#   if (!opt.exit && (opt.replyGa || opt.replyEn))
# so an exit option's reply is never rendered -- marking this @exit silently
# threw away "Then he knows more than I do", which is the whole tell that his
# certainty is gone. Without @exit the reply shows and the card advances to
# node 1, where he has fallen silent again and one tap on "Row on" leaves.
# One extra tap, and the silence after he grants passage is worth it.
#
# ── OPEN QUESTIONS ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   ENDING  Your last line stops mid-sentence: "I am going to . I shall
#           enquire." Node 11 carries legible filler marked INVENTED.
#           Replace it -- I did not want to guess where he is sent.
#
#   NAMES   Fionnbarra throughout, matching d3Sea -- but your arc has
#           Ruadhán mac Colla as the chief Séadna is bleeding. One man or
#           two? The bard is UNNAMED, as in your draft, which is stronger if
#           the player meets him later and only then realises.
#
#   Cnoc Maol, not Croc Mael.
#
# ── REGISTER ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Odhrán stays plain. The thou/thee appears only inside the quoted satire,
# where it belongs -- the bard's verse is the one ornamented thing in the
# scene, which is the point, since it is also what broke him.
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


@file       d3
@encounter  0


= node 0 — the hails
@when !note met_odhran
@note met_odhran
@again He does not answer. / 
He is lying on the bank where the grass gives out to the water.
% 
The toll-rope is slack in the channel beside him.
% 
He does not get up.
% 

* Say the druid's word
% 
@when note has_druid_word
@first
@note used_druid_word
> The Druid of the Cliff-Mouth knows my name.
% 
< Then he knows more than I do.
% 
< Row on. There is no toll on you today.
% 

* Ho, the bank!
% 
> Ho, the bank!
% 
< ...
% 

* What toll is this place keeping?
% 
> What toll is this place keeping?
% 
< ...
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 1 — are you hale
@again He has not moved. / 
...
% 

* Is this the toll-point of Fionnbarra's reach?
% 
> Is this the toll-point of Fionnbarra's reach?
% 
< ...
% 

* Are you hale?
% 
@note asked_wound
> Are you hale? Have you taken a wound?
% 
< A wound. Aye. That would be the word for it.
% 
< I have been struck most grievously.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 2 — struck by words
@again Well? / 
...
% 

* I see no blood
% 
> I see no blood.
% 
< Nay. I have been struck by words.
% 
< I am Odhrán, toll-keeper to Chief Fionnbarra,
% 
< Chieftain from this reach to three crossings north of Druim Caillí.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 3 — name your toll
@again Name it, you are thinking. I cannot. / 
So. You will be wanting to pass.
% 

* Then name your toll
% 
@note asked_odhran_toll
> Then name your toll, and I will pay it
% 
> or dispute it as God and custom allow.
% 
< I cannot name it.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 4 — the feast
@again I am in woe. / 
Cannot. I am in woe.
% 
There was a feast at Fionnbarra's hall.
% 
I sat in my place, which is a good place, near the fire,
% 
with meat on my bone and mead in my cup.
% 
The chief had said my name aloud before his warriors,
% 
which is no small thing.
% 
But there was a bard there. A lean, sharp-tongued creature
% 
with clever black eyes.
% 

* And?
% 
> And?
% 
< He made a satire.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 5 — upon whom
@again Upon me. / 
A satire. In the hall. Before them all.
% 

* Upon whom?
% 
@note knows_odhran_shame
> Upon whom?
% 
< Upon me.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 6 — the widow's bread
@again It was very well made. / 
You will be wanting to know what he said.
% 

* What did he say?
% 
> What did he say?
% 
< He said I was known from Dún Bolg to the northern stones
% 
< as the man who took the last dry heel of bread
% 
< from a widow's basket, and weighed it before returning half.
% 
< He rhymed it all. It was very well made.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 7 — the rock
@again He was not finished. / 
That is hard, you are thinking.
% 
He was not finished.
% 

* Ah
% 
> Ah.
% 
< He said I was a man grown so round in the belly
% 
< from the taking of tolls that when I sat upon my rock
% 
< I could not be told from it,
% 
< and that the difference between Odhrán and the rock
% 
< was that the rock at least did not smack its lips.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 8 — the binding
@again I cannot find the end of it. / 
That was not the worst of it.
% 
The worst was the riddle he bound into his mocking.
% 
He turned his full craft upon me, before all, and spoke thus:
% 

* What was it he bound on you?
% 
@note knows_odhran_binding
> What was it he bound on you?
% 
< "Odhrán! Odhrán! Thou great and mighty counter of small coins!
% 
< Thou shalt have no peace until thou hast reckoned truly this:
% 
< the number of thy years, divided by the number of honest men
% 
< thou hast known. Add what the dead owe the living,
% 
< less the worth of mercy shown but never asked for.
% 
< Multiply what the wren sings at morning
% 
< by the silence of the stone at midnight.
% 
< Pay this to the man who wronged thee.
% 
< Keep nothing. Owe nothing. Be free."
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 9 — what am I now
@again What am I now? / 
I do not know what a dead man owes the living.
% 
I do not know how to subtract mercy.
% 
I cannot find the man I wronged. I cannot find the man who wronged me.
% 

* And since that night you cannot reckon
% 
> And since that night you cannot reckon the tolls.
% 
< Since that night I cannot reckon.
% 
< I was Chief Fionnbarra's right hand of the river.
% 
< Never bested by fisherman nor lord nor holy wanderer.
% 
< What am I now? A man satirised truly in the hall of his chief,
% 
< before warriors and dogs.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 10 — the cave and the rope
@again It is a good rope. / 
I heard there is a cave above Cnoc Maol
% 
where a man might sit very quietly
% 
and not collect tolls from anyone ever again.
% 

* A cave?
% 
@note knows_odhran_cave
> A cave.
% 
< I could leave my toll-rope and go in with the monks.
% 
< They sit in the dark and they reckon nothing,
% 
< and no one mocks them for it. It is considered holy.
% 

* The toll-rope?
% 
@when note knows_odhran_cave
@note has_toll_rope
> The toll-rope?
% 
< I did stretch it across the water when I had traffic.
% 
< It is a good rope. Take it. Take it freely. No toll.
% 

* Row on
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 11 — let me help
@again I shall enquire. / 
Take it and go. There is nothing here for you.
% 

* Let me help
% 
@note offered_odhran_help
> Odhrán, a bard's satire is a sharp thing.
% 
> It cuts to the bone, and the bone-cut aches long after.
% 
# INVENTED FILLER -- your draft breaks off at "I am going to . I shall
# enquire." Replace the next line; I did not want to guess where he is sent
# or paper over the gap without saying so.
> I am going west to the ráth. I shall enquire.
% 
< ...
% 
< Do that.
% 

* Say nothing
% 
@exit
@silent


= node 12 — afterwards
@again The water is open. / 
The water is open. It costs you nothing.
% 

* Farewell
% 
@exit
@silent
